0 Remark: Keep in mind that very small fruiting bodies and buttons are often entirely deep blue-green, regardless of the species (phenomenon observed in Lactarius salmonicolor, L. deliciosus, L. quieticolor and L. deterrimus).011140_remarque0
0 Latex white; species growing with Larix 011250 Lactarius porninsis *0
0 Latex orange, red or blue; species growing with other coniferous trees0113602
0 Pileus entirely salmon orange, sometimes with a paler margin, without green tinges; green tinges seldom-ly present in very young or very old basidiocarps; species associated with Abies; pleuromacrocystidia very abundant and large (up to 96 µm long) 023470 Lactarius salmonicolor *0
0 Pileus mostly not purely orange but with green, blue, brownish, greyish or red tinges; discolouring green when bruised or when older; species associated with Pinus or Picea; pleuromacrocystidia max. 70 µm long, often quite rare0235803
0 Latex blue in the major part of the fruiting body but orange in the lower half of the stipe; stipe with clear blue tinges; species restricted to the mediterranean region; associated with Pinus halepensis 035690 Lactarius cyanopus *0
0 Latex orange or red, rarely with a bluish or greenish zone right underneath the pileipellis; stipe mainly orange, reddish or with green tinges; not restricted to the mediterranean region; associated with Pinus or Picea03571004
0 Latex red when exuded; lamellae pinkish to vinaceous red; associated with Pinus04781105
0 Latex orange when exuded; lamellae orange; associated with Pinus or Picea04791206
0 Pileus and stipe with orange tinges; pileus generally azonate; pileus slowly and relatively slightly discolouring greenish; stipe cylindrical or rarely tapering downwards; lamellae pinkish 05810130 Lactarius sanguifluus *0
0 Pileus and stipe with pale to deep vinaceous red tinges; pileus generally clearly zonate; pileus strongly discolouring greenish after picking the basidiocarps; stipe mostly short and strongly tapering downwards; lamellae pale violet to vinaceous 05811140 Lactarius vinosus *0
0 Orange latex becoming quickly vinaceous red (in 5 to 8 min.); often with a lot of green in pileus, especially in older specimens 06912150 Lactarius semisanguifluus *0
0 Latex remaining orange for more than 10–15 min.; some green may be present in the pileus, especially in bruised spots, but basidiocarp never entirely greenish069131607
0 Stipe without (obvious) scrobicules; species associated with Picea; spore ornamentation consisting of isolated warts and short ridges, never reticulate0713141708
0 Stipe mostly with scrobicules; species associated with Pinus; spores ornamented with an incomplete reticulum0713151809
0 Pileus typically azonate or with a few zones near the margin, with mainly bright orange colours and green tinges; stipe unicolourous bright orange without scrobicules but often with green hues; cheilomacrocystidia mostly quite abundant; species common everywhere in Europe 081416190 Lactarius deterrimus *0
0 Pileus very clearly zonate and brown-orange with sometimes lilac-grey tinges; stipe pallid to dull orange-buff, rarely with unclear scrobicules; cheilomacrocystidia mostly rare; a subboreal, boreal and subalpine species 081417200 Lactarius fennoscandicus *0
0 Latex orange, remaining orange or changing slowly to red in more than 30 min.; pileus orange, mostly with a whitish aspect, mostly clearly zonate; stipe clearly scrobiculate; species occurring on neutral to calcareous soil; spores not very heavily ornamented 091518210 Lactarius deliciosus *0
0 Latex orange, changing to red in about 20 min.; pileus colour highly variable, from orange to brownish and sometimes bluish, but mostly dull; stipe almost without or with inconspicuous scrobicules; species growing on acidic soil; spores ornamented with very thick ridges 091519220 Lactarius quieticolor *0
